From a6cb35f087fc9a8dee88a43ca1f9f14464657f55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xalpharush <0xalpharush@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] msan: a sealed start-up
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Sealed init (patch 0012) made the common runtime read no environment and no
/proc, but MSan's own start-up still took paths that break the sealed contract:
it installed its signal handlers, a detection exited with a status the program
could have returned rather than dying by a signal, it re-execed on an unlimited
stack, and it mapped its fixed layout with MAP_FIXED over whatever was already
there.  This gives MSan the sealed start-up ASan already has, mechanism for
mechanism, in MSan's own code: sharing asan's would rebuild the asan runtime
and move the archive md5 the OSS-Fuzz proofs record.

Layout reservation (msan.cpp, msan_linux.cpp).  Unsealed MSan dry-runs the
layout with MemoryRangeIsAvailable, re-execs if it conflicts, then MAP_FIXEDs
its shadow.  In sealed mode MemoryRangeIsAvailable answers yes unasked (0012),
so the dry run cannot fail and the MAP_FIXED covers whatever the program or
kernel had already placed in a shadow range -- a placement the specification
these binaries are verified against admits.  SealedReserveShadow instead
reserves every SHADOW and mapped-ORIGIN range readable-writable and every
INVALID and unmapped-ORIGIN range PROT_NONE, each with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, and
spins on refusal via the common SealedHangOnAllocationFailure -- the shape of
0019's SealedReserveShadow for ASan.  The rule for each range is one helper,
LayoutRangeAction, whose action covers every range type (map, protect,
allocator, app); InitShadow and the sealed reservation both switch on it, so
the two cannot disagree.

Three things differ from asan and all three matter:

  - The ALLOCATOR range is reserved as well, PROT_NONE.  MSan's primary
    allocator has a fixed base (msan_allocator.cpp: 0x700000000000 on x86_64,
    0xE00000000000 on aarch64), which it maps with MAP_FIXED; asan's kernel
    picks its base.  Left open, a mapping the kernel placed in that range was
    silently replaced: the runtime's own first pages, or the [vdso] and [vvar].
    Reserved, such a mapping is a refusal (the spin), and the allocator's
    MAP_FIXED lands on the runtime's own reservation.  The allocator also
    maps its region-info array, AdditionalSize() bytes (8 KiB on x86_64), with
    MAP_FIXED at the end of its space -- in the APP range just above the
    ALLOCATOR one -- so that range is reserved PROT_NONE too.
    msan_allocator.cpp reports it (MsanAllocatorRegionInfoRange), from the
    allocator's own parameters and SizeClassAllocator64::AdditionalSize(), so
    the shared allocator header is not touched.

  - The reservation is made at the top of __msan_init, right after the frame is
    recorded and before AvoidCVE_2016_2143(), as AsanInitInternal reserves
    asan's shadow before the flag parser takes the runtime's first page.  It
    used to be made inside InitShadowWithReExec, after CacheBinaryName(),
    InitializeFlags() and InitializeInterceptors() had already made unhinted
    mappings -- which the kernel could put inside the allocator range.
    __msan_get_track_origins() reads a link-time symbol, not a flag, so it is
    known there.  SealedReserveShadow is called once, there, and a sealed run
    does not call InitShadowWithReExec at all.

  - The symbolizer is not created at start-up.  __msan_init used to call
    Symbolizer::GetOrInit()->AddHooks(...) unconditionally, and creating the
    symbolizer chooses a tool, which looks for llvm-symbolizer on the
    filesystem: a newfstatat on every sealed run.  AsanInitInternal skips its
    symbolizer initialization when sealed for the same reason.  The hooks are
    what keep MSan from checking the symbolizer's own memory, so they cannot
    simply be dropped: MsanEnsureSymbolizerHooks creates the symbolizer and
    installs them once, and is called from __msan_init when unsealed (as
    before) and, sealed or not, from GET_FATAL_STACK_TRACE_PC_BP and
    GET_FATAL_STACK_TRACE_IF_EMPTY, which every MSan report, CHECK failure and
    __sanitizer_print_stack_trace passes through before it symbolizes.  A
    sealed run therefore looks for a symbolizer only once it is reporting,
    which is outside the seal, as for asan.

SealFlags (msan.cpp).  ASan pins a set of sanitizer-neutral common flags in
asan_flags.cpp's SealFlags(); MSan had none.  The same common flags are pinned
at the end of InitializeFlags, after __msan_default_options() and MSAN_OPTIONS
are parsed so a program hook cannot reset them: handle_* = kHandleSignalNo and
use_sigaltstack off (no signal handler); abort_on_error on (a detection dies by
SIGABRT, not an exit status the program could have returned); hard/soft
rss_limit_mb 0 (no RSS-limit thread); heap_profile, detect_leaks off;
allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms -1; and clear_shadow_mmap_threshold at its
maximum, for asan's reason: above that threshold MSan's SetShadow zeroes a
shadow range by mapping fresh pages over it with MAP_FIXED, which in the model
is a new grant carved out of the shadow mapping, while the madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)
patch 0020 allows must lie inside one grant.  asan also pins
protect_shadow_gap, which MSan does not have.  Three flags asan's seal leaves
alone are pinned too, all already off by default, so a hook cannot turn on a
syscall the seal rules out: compress_stack_depot, print_module_map,
decorate_proc_maps.  The values are duplicated from asan's seal rather than
shared, on purpose.

ReExec (msan.cpp).  MSan also re-execs when the stack limit is unlimited.  In
sealed mode GetArgsAndEnv returns empty, so that ReExec would run /proc/self/exe
with an empty argv and environment; it is gated off, as the dry-run re-exec is.
With an unlimited stack the kernel's legacy bottom-up layout can start inside
MSan's shadow, and the reservation then refuses: the sealed spin, which
README.md's "Known limitation" describes.

Whether a sealed run gets past this reservation depends on where the kernel
put its own start-up mappings; how often it spins, and on which hosts, is in
README.md's "Known limitation".  At vm.mmap_rnd_bits=32 the first version of
this patch, which reserved inside InitShadowWithReExec and left the allocator
range open, died by SIGSEGV on some runs; this version does not.

strace, sealed.  A clean run makes no newfstatat or open for llvm-symbolizer;
an uninitialized-read run looks for it once, after the report has begun, and
prints one report.  For a program that does malloc(8 MiB), memset and free,
every MAP_FIXED after the start-up reservation lands on a mapping the runtime
or libc made for itself: the allocator's 4 TiB and its 8 KiB region-info array
onto the PROT_NONE reservations made for them, 128 KiB inside an 8 MiB
PROT_NONE range the runtime reserved, and musl's brk guard page, which the
uninstrumented program makes too.  None lands in a shadow or origin range, and
the freed block's shadow is handed back with one madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) inside
the shadow reservation.  With the first version of this patch the same
program's trace had three 8 MiB MAP_FIXED mappings inside shadow-3: the
unpoisoning of the sealed main-thread stack window at start-up, and two for the
allocation.
